<exercisegroup cols="2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Solutions as geometric points of intersection</title>

<introduction><p>
	For each system of equations,
	use a graphing tool to graph the corresponding lines on the set same of axes.
	Use the plot to determine if the system is
	<term><xref ref="definition-systems-consistent" text="custom">consistent</xref></term>
	or
	<term><xref ref="definition-systems-inconsistent" text="custom">inconsistent</xref></term>.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
				x \amp + \amp 4 y \amp = \amp 6 \\
				x \amp - \amp 2 y \amp = \amp 0 \\
			3 x \amp + \amp y \amp = \amp 7
		\end{sysofeqns}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution>
		<image width="32.6%">
			<latex-image label="image-systems-exercises-system-of-lines-1">
				<xi:include href="system-of-lines-1.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription>Three lines intersecting at a single point in common.</shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				The graphs of three lines are plotted on the same set of <m>xy</m>-axes.
				One line has a slightly positive slope,
				another has a slightly negative slope,
				and the third has a very negative slope.
				All three lines meet at a common point of intersection in the first quadrant.
			</p></description>
		</image>
		<p>
			The three lines intersect at a common point,
			and so the system is consistent.
			Your graphing tool may tell you that this common point is <m>(2,1)</m>,
			and you may verify that this is correct algebraically by confirming that <m>x = 2, y = 1</m> constitutes a solution to the system.
		</p>
	</solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
				x \amp - \amp   y \amp = \amp -2 \\
			2 x \amp + \amp   y \amp = \amp  5 \\
				x \amp + \amp 2 y \amp = \amp  1
		\end{sysofeqns}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution>
		<image width="45%">
			<latex-image label="image-systems-exercises-system-of-lines-2">
				<xi:include href="system-of-lines-2.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription>Three intersecting lines that do not intersect at a single point in common.</shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				The graphs of three lines are plotted on the same set of <m>xy</m>-axes.
				One line has a moderately positive slope,
				another has a moderately negative slope,
				and the third has a very negative slope.
				The first and third lines intersect at a point in the first quadrant,
				the first and second lines intersect at a point in the second quadrant,
				and the second and third lines intersect at a point in the fourth quadrant.
				However, there is no point of common intersection for all three lines.
			</p></description>
		</image>
		<p>
			While these lines intersect in pairs,
			there is no common point where all three intersect.
			Therefore, the system is inconsistent.
		</p>
	</solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
